{"id":"jpn-t0-0001","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.hello","domain":1,"text_native":"もしもし","text_romanized":"moshi moshi","text_english":"Hello.","source_caption":"Hello","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The default greeting anywhere. Works with every age and every status.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0002","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.morning","domain":1,"text_native":"お早うございます。","text_romanized":"Ohayō gozaimasu","text_english":"Good morning.","source_caption":"Good morning.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used until roughly midday. Getting the time of day wrong is noticed; not saying it is only noticed by someone expecting it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0003","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.afternoon","domain":1,"text_native":"こんにちは。","text_romanized":"Konnichiwa","text_english":"Good afternoon.","source_caption":"Good afternoon.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The middle of the day. In several languages this is the same word as the evening greeting, and using it late is not an error.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0004","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.evening","domain":1,"text_native":"こんばんは。","text_romanized":"Kombanwa","text_english":"Good evening.","source_caption":"Good evening.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"From late afternoon. Often interchangeable with the afternoon greeting.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0005","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.how-are-you","domain":1,"text_native":"お元気ですか？","text_romanized":"O-genki desu ka?","text_english":"How are you?","source_caption":"How are you?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The opening move in almost every conversation. In most of these languages it is a greeting rather than a question and does not expect a literal answer.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0006","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.i-am-well","domain":1,"text_native":"はい、元気です。","text_romanized":"Hai, genki desu","text_english":"I am well, thank you.","source_caption":"Fine, thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The expected answer to the greeting above. Answering it wrong reads as evasive in cultures where it is a formula.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0007","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.nice-to-meet-you","domain":1,"text_native":"始めまして。どうぞ宜しくお願いします。","text_romanized":"Hajimemashite. Dōzo yoroshiku onegaishimasu","text_english":"Nice to meet you.","source_caption":"Nice to meet you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said on a first introduction. In several languages there is a separate phrase for meeting someone formally that a stranger will use instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0008","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.what-is-your-name","domain":1,"text_native":"お名前は？","text_romanized":"O-namae wa?","text_english":"What is your name?","source_caption":"What's your name? (lit. \"Your name is...\")","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"You get asked this before you get to ask it. Recognising the question is the difference between answering and freezing.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0009","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.thank-you","domain":1,"text_native":"どうもありがとうございました。","text_romanized":"Dōmo arigatō gozaimashita","text_english":"Thank you.","source_caption":"Thank you very much.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The single most useful word in any of these languages. Saying it costs nothing and changes how everything after it is received.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0010","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.youre-welcome","domain":1,"text_native":"どういたしまして。","text_romanized":"Dō itashimashite","text_english":"You are welcome.","source_caption":"You're welcome.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"You will be handed it constantly, and there is no way to avoid replying. In a few languages the reply is the same word as 'yes'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0011","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.excuse-me","domain":1,"text_native":"すみません。","text_romanized":"Sumimasen","text_english":"Excuse me.","source_caption":"Excuse me.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Two different things in one phrase: getting someone's attention, and getting past them. Guessing wrong is jarring — one version walks through a conversation.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0012","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.sorry","domain":1,"text_native":"ごめんなさい。","text_romanized":"Gomen nasai","text_english":"I am sorry.","source_caption":"I'm sorry.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Apologising for a small fault — bumping someone, being late, not understanding. The form you use signals how much of a fault you think it was.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0013","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.one-moment","domain":1,"text_native":"ちょっとお待ちください。","text_romanized":"Chotto omachi kudasai","text_english":"One moment, please.","source_caption":"One moment, please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Buys you time to think, find a word, or finish what you were doing — without the silence that reads as rude or as not understanding.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0014","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.goodbye","domain":1,"text_native":"じゃね。","text_romanized":"Ja ne","text_english":"Goodbye.","source_caption":"Goodbye.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you leave. Leaving without one is noticeably rude in most of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0015","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"分かりません。","text_romanized":"Wakarimasen","text_english":"I do not understand.","source_caption":"I don't understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The one phrase that keeps a conversation alive. Without it people stop trying, and with it they often rephrase in simpler words.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0016","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"repair.speak-slower","domain":12,"text_native":"ゆっくり話してください。","text_romanized":"Yukkuri hanashite kudasai","text_english":"Please speak more slowly.","source_caption":"Please speak slowly.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Asks for the thing you actually need. Saying it is nearly always received well, even badly pronounced, and asking again is not.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0017","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.speak-english","domain":12,"text_native":"英語が話せますか？","text_romanized":"Eigo ga hanasemasu ka?","text_english":"Do you speak English?","source_caption":"Do you speak English?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Asked of you constantly, and the question that decides whether anything after it can happen. There is a polite version and a blunt version and the gap between them is wide.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"jpn-t0-0018","lang":"jpn","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.little-english","domain":12,"text_native":"はい、少し。","text_romanized":"Hai, sukoshi","text_english":"A little English.","source_caption":"Yes, a little.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The answer to the question above, and the thing that tells a speaker to switch to simpler words rather than to English.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Japanese phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Japanese_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
